title: 'Audit P1: isolation, write-back safety, and durability — with the red-first discipline finally biting'
summary: |
  Closed D-7 (--root), C-5 (per-path dirty guard), C-7 (fsync), and C-15 (staging-name collisions) across two agents running in parallel on disjoint files. 813 passing, zero skipped, up from 749. The batch's real value was evidential rather than mechanical: each fix was reverted in source and the failures recorded, which reproduced Arky's "writer A's rename published writer B's value" verbatim — and caught one of the new tests being vacuous, passing both before and after. Warpline also sealed a strand for the commit that fixed its own merge path.</summary>
  <body>## What the batch actually closed

  **D-7** was the cheapest item in the audit and the one with the widest blast radius: no `--root`, no `WARPLINE_ROOT`, and a `process.cwd()` fallback that fires only when git fails, so every command targeted the live fabric by default. That is why all six auditors had to be given explicit `os.tmpdir()` discipline, and it was a hard prerequisite for the F4 runner whose fixtures live in-repo. Fixed with a real chokepoint (`src/root.ts`), not 31 edits — a per-site fix is how the 32nd site gets missed, and duplicated root resolution is why the defect existed at all.

  **C-5** was mostly wiring: the correct policy already existed at `restore.ts:60-68` and two call sites bypassed it. The substantive part was Judge's sharper point — the guard tested directory emptiness, which an in-place restore can never satisfy, so `--force` became mandatory for the normal case and the guard trained its own bypass. Per-path is what turns it from theatre into a guard.

  **C-7** produced the best-evidenced decision of the session. There was no fsync anywhere, so "sealed → exit 0" was a claim about the page cache. The builder could not choose between plain fsync and F_FULLFSYNC because on macOS Node's fsync IS F_FULLFSYNC — libuv routes both fsync and fdatasync there — and it proved this by cost signature (3.66ms vs 0.17ms unhardened) rather than asserting it. Hardware flush or nothing, hence an on/off knob rather than git's core.fsyncMethod. Cost measured, not assumed: +22-44ms on an incremental seal (immaterial), 10.2x on a 2,000-object cold genesis (very material). Batch-fsync recorded as a follow-up instead of improvised.

  **C-15** fixed four durable writers sharing a `${p}.tmp`, where a racing writer could publish another's value AND return success underneath the CAS — because the CAS runs before the write. The ref-residue half was fixed better than specified: composing an explicit residue check into `isRefName` rather than banning dots in the regex, so a legitimately dotted ref name still works.

  ## The evidential turn

  Across the P0 batch I asked four times for red-first confirmations and never got them, because the builder truncated mid-report each time. This batch delivered them, and they earned their keep immediately:

  - Reverting C-15 reproduced Arky's D-B verbatim: `expected 'pick:v2:bbbb…' to be 'pick:v2:aaaa…'` — writer A's rename publishing writer B's value.
  - Reverting C-7 turned 7 of 14 tests red; the 6 that stayed green assert the ABSENCE of fsync, which is the correct split and is itself evidence the assertions bite where they should.
  - The builder found one of its OWN new tests was vacuous — a syncDir ENOENT case that passed both before and after the tightening — and replaced it with a POSIX EACCES case that fails against the old code.

  That last one is the whole thesis of this audit, occurring inside the audit's own remediation. A test written after a fix proves the code compiles.

  ## Parallel agents: works, with one rule

  Two builders on disjoint file sets, each told which four files the other owned and instructed to report — never fix — failures outside its own scope. The isolation agent correctly identified the only tsc errors as the durability agent's in-flight work and left them alone. Without that rule the predictable failure is one agent "fixing" the other's half-written code, producing a state neither can explain. The cost is that no intermediate test number is trustworthy; the coordinator has to do the authoritative run.

  ## A pleasing verification

  The fabric grew 64 to 65 strands mid-batch. `head -64 | md5` is byte-identical to the pre-audit baseline, and strand 65 is the post-commit hook sealing commit 7a9a555b — the commit that fixed Warpline's own merge path. The tool recorded the change that repaired it, and the isolation discipline held across all eight agents.</body>
