  <learnings>["Fix duplicated logic with a chokepoint, not N edits. Root resolution was duplicated across ~31 sites, which IS why D-7 existed; patching each site would have left the next one to reintroduce it. Same shape as the reserved-name normalizer in P0.", "A guard that cannot be satisfied in the normal case trains its own bypass. The dirty-destination check tested directory emptiness, which an in-place restore can never pass, so --force became routine and the guard protected nothing. Per-path made it real.", "Measure the cost of a safety default rather than asserting it is fine. fsync is +3.4% on an incremental seal and 10.2x on a cold genesis — the same change, two completely different verdicts, and only the numbers reveal that the escape hatch is load-bearing rather than decorative.", "Reverting the fix and re-running is the only proof a test is non-vacuous, and it pays for itself: it reproduced an auditor's finding verbatim AND exposed a brand-new test that passed both before and after. Ask for the failure text, not the assurance.", "Parallel builders on disjoint files work if and only if each is told to REPORT rather than FIX failures outside its scope. Otherwise one agent adapts to the other's half-written code. The trade is that no intermediate suite number is trustworthy — the coordinator must run the authoritative pass."]</learnings>
